  "evaluation": "C is best: it recognizes that Study B reports improvement rather than harm and grounds the apparent disagreement in a depression dimension of a broader quality-of-life measure versus a full depression scale. B reaches the same appropriate verdict and reason, though its emphasis on opposite scale directions is less directly aligned with the rules than the difference in what the instruments measure. A incorrectly labels the disagreement genuine despite acknowledging that the findings are not clearly opposed; study-design differences do not establish conflicting results.",
